1	// ResumeDriver translates a hybrid_state_v1 into a sequence of DMI register
2	// ops against the RISC-V Debug Module. The orchestration is testable in
3	// isolation by injecting a recording bus.
4	//
5	// GPR transport complete: the driver restores GPRs x1..x31 and DPC. These
6	// cases pin the halt/resume handshake, the x1..x31 program (one COMMAND per
7	// register, x0 skipped), the DPC write, that M-mode CSRs/PMP are NOT programmed
8	// via DM abstract at this stage, and the header guard.
9	//
10	// FP restore, complete: under HYBRID_FLAG_FP the driver first writes mstatus
11	// (force FS = Initial so the abstract FPR writes are accepted even when the
12	// drained field is Off), then programs f0..f31 via abstract commands and
13	// restores fcsr (frm + sticky fflags, L20) under the same FS gate. With FP
14	// clear the driver issues no FPR or fcsr write. B17 pins the cmderr
15	// diagnostic side channel; B9 pins that every abstract write busy-polls
16	// abstractcs before the next caller inspects cmderr.
17	//
18	// Base M-mode CSR restore, complete: after the GPRs/DPC the driver restores
19	// mtvec + mscratch (L23), the writable trap/interrupt CSRs mepc/mcause/mtval/
20	// mie/mip (L24), and the Andes cache-setup CSRs mcache_ctl/mmisc_ctl -- all via
21	// the tolerant CSR path (write + clear cmderr, no PB fallback) -- plus mcycle/
22	// minstret (F-T2.4) so the vsim phase keeps counting from QEMU1's drain.
